<body>

<h1>SecureU — Teacher Terminal Output & Lab Presentation Guide</h1>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> Java Programming / DBMS Lab &bull; <strong>Powered by:</strong> Java JDK 27 + MySQL JDBC (java.sql.*)</p>

<h2>Method 1: One-Click Terminal Launcher (Recommended)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open the project folder: <code>JAVA PROJECT</code></li>
  <li>Double-click <strong><code>Launch-Terminal-Console.bat</code></strong></li>
  <li>A Command Prompt window immediately opens and displays:
    <ul>
      <li>Application Banner with JDK version and MySQL connection status.</li>
      <li>Live MySQL Metrics Overview (Total students, complaints, breakdown).</li>
      <li>Formatted ASCII Complaints Table fetched via JDBC.</li>
      <li>Interactive Console Menu (commands 1 to 8).</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Method 2: From PowerShell or Command Prompt</h2>
<pre>
cd "C:\Users\Hariprasad\Videos\JAVA PROJECT"
java -jar CollegeComplaint.jar --terminal
</pre>

<h2>Terminal Commands Available During Viva Demonstration</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width:15%">Command</th>
      <th style="width:35%">Action</th>
      <th style="width:50%">What the Teacher Sees</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>1</strong></td>
      <td>View All Complaints Table</td>
      <td>Full formatted ASCII table containing complaint ID, student name, department, category, status, and description.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>2</strong></td>
      <td>Filter by Status</td>
      <td>Filter grievances by <em>Pending</em>, <em>In Progress</em>, or <em>Resolved</em> directly in terminal.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>3</strong></td>
      <td>View Registered Students</td>
      <td>ASCII table of student IDs, names, departments, emails, and contact phone numbers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>4</strong></td>
      <td>Submit Complaint via Terminal</td>
      <td>Interactive prompts to enter student ID, category, and issue. Executes SQL <code>INSERT</code> via <code>PreparedStatement</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>5</strong></td>
      <td>Update Status via Terminal</td>
      <td>Prompts for complaint ID, new status, and remarks. Executes SQL <code>UPDATE</code> in MySQL.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>6</strong></td>
      <td>Database Metrics Overview</td>
      <td>Live SQL <code>COUNT(*)</code> calculations printed directly to the console.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>7</strong></td>
      <td>Open Web GUI</td>
      <td>Launches browser at <code>http://localhost:3000</code> while keeping the console running.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Live Real-Time JDBC Logging in Terminal</h2>
<p>When the web GUI is used simultaneously, every action streams live logs into the terminal window:</p>
<pre>
[21:12:55] [JDBC LOGIN   ] SUCCESS: Student 'Sneha S' (sneha@example.com) authenticated.
[21:12:55] [JDBC SELECT  ] Fetched 50 complaints from MySQL for Dashboard view.
[21:13:10] [JDBC INSERT  ] New complaint filed! ID #51 | Student ID: 2 | Category: Academic
[21:13:25] [JDBC UPDATE  ] Complaint #51 status updated to 'Resolved' | Remarks: 'Fixed'
</pre>

<h2>Common Lab Exam & Viva Questions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Q: Which Java packages are used?</strong><br>
  <em>A:</em> <code>java.sql.*</code> (<code>Connection</code>, <code>PreparedStatement</code>, <code>Statement</code>, <code>ResultSet</code>, <code>DriverManager</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Q: How are SQL injection attacks prevented?</strong><br>
  <em>A:</em> By using parameterized queries with <code>PreparedStatement</code> and <code>?</code> placeholders.</li>
  <li><strong>Q: Where is MySQL Connector/J located?</strong><br>
  <em>A:</em> Inside <code>lib/mysql-connector-j.jar</code>, specified in <code>manifest.txt</code>.</li>
</ul>

</body>
